.TH CRUNCH-LD 1 "November 2026" "crunch-ld 0.9.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
crunch-ld \- an experimental linker for x86-64 Linux ELF
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B crunch-ld
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIfiles...\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B crunch-ld
is a lightweight, independent ELF linker designed for x86-64 Linux.
It accepts ELF64 x86-64 relocatable objects and regular static archives.
Static executable output is the default. Dynamic and shared output also
accept supported ELF shared objects. Input and archive order is significant.
This experimental linker is not a complete replacement for GNU ld.
For dynamic and shared links, canonical x86 IBT/SHSTK GNU property notes
are discarded with a warning; the output does not retain those CET protections.
Other GNU property notes are rejected. Relocatable links preserve input notes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-shared, \-\-shared
Produce a restricted, loadable shared object. GOT-relative data references and
PLT calls support undefined and preemptible symbols, resolved eagerly by the
dynamic loader. Initialized 64-bit pointers in writable sections are resolved
by the loader, including symbol addends and interposition. Direct PC-relative
references must target local, hidden, or protected definitions. Read-only
dynamic relocations are unsupported. TLS, unwind information, initialization and
finalization through legacy sections, and full symbol versioning are unsupported.
Shared initialization and finalization arrays are supported, including numeric
priorities. See the crunch-ld README for input restrictions. ELF64 x86-64
shared inputs with dynamic symbol and section metadata are recorded as
dependencies, using their SONAME or, if absent, the input path (the basename
for a library found with \-l). Repeated dependency names are recorded once.
The loader search path can be embedded with \-rpath or configured separately.
See the crunch-ld README for details.
.TP
.B \-dynamic, \-\-dynamic
Produce a restricted fixed-address dynamically linked executable, using
the interpreter selected by configure by default. Direct shared inputs are recorded as
dependencies, and GOT and PLT references are resolved eagerly. Strong
undefined references must be supplied by direct shared inputs. Executable
initialization arrays are invoked before the entry point, with priorities and
the original argc, argv, and envp. Executable finalization arrays run in reverse
order when the reserved __crunch_run_finalizers hook is called. Current Crunch
main-return and exit() paths call it; custom entry points must call it themselves.
Direct exit syscalls bypass it, and shared-library finalizers, standard libc
startup, TLS, and unwind sections remain unsupported. See AUTOMATIC RUNTIME
and the crunch-ld README for details.
.TP
.B \-pie, \-\-pie, \-fPIE, \-fpie
With \-dynamic, produce a position-independent executable using the system
loader. Image-relative 64-bit pointers in writable sections are relocated at
load time. Dynamic text relocations, narrow absolute image addresses, and
direct PC-relative references to shared data are rejected. Most synthetic
linker symbols are unsupported in dynamic PIE. Without \-dynamic, experimental
PIE output has no dynamic loader and rejects GOT relocations.
.TP
.B \-I PATH, \-\-dynamic-linker PATH
Select dynamic executable mode and use the absolute interpreter PATH.
.TP
.B \-soname NAME, \-\-soname=NAME, \-h NAME
Set the SONAME of a shared output.
.TP
.B \-rpath PATH, \-\-rpath PATH
Embed a DT_RUNPATH in shared or dynamic executable output. The forms
\-rpath=PATH and \-\-rpath=PATH are also supported. Repeated paths are joined
with colons. Quote '$ORIGIN' to preserve it for the loader. RUNPATH applies to
the containing object's direct dependencies, not their children. It does not
affect link-time library search. An automatically selected shared Crunch runtime
adds its directory after explicit paths. Legacy DT_RPATH is not emitted. Empty arguments are rejected; empty
colon-separated components can search the current working directory.
.TP
.B \-z relro, \-zrelro
For shared or dynamic executable output, emit PT_GNU_RELRO covering dynamic
metadata, the GOT, initialization and finalization arrays, and writable
\&.data.rel.ro input sections. A compatible loader makes these pages read-only
after relocation; ordinary data, BSS, and Crunch runtime registries remain
writable. Without this option RELRO is not emitted. Other \-z options are
unsupported.
.TP
.B \-\-no-undefined
For shared output, reject strong symbols referenced by relocations but not
defined by input objects or direct shared dependencies. Undefined weak
symbols remain permitted.
.TP
.B \-Wabi-mismatch, \-Wall
Warn about differing Crunch IR function signatures in declarations and
selected relocatable-object definitions, including extracted archive members,
and visible candidates in directly linked shared libraries.
This is best effort, may report unused declarations or compatible calling
conventions, and does not verify missing metadata, indirect dependencies, or
later-loaded libraries. Runtime symbol interposition is not fully modeled;
absence of a warning does not establish compatibility.
.TP
.B \-\-forbid-defined=SYMBOL
Reject an output-object definition of SYMBOL, including a common or weak
definition, while allowing definitions in shared inputs. May be repeated.
.TP
.B \-\-unique-shared-symbol=SYMBOL
Reject SYMBOL if it is defined by more than one distinct direct shared input.
Transitive dependencies are not checked. May be repeated.
.TP
.B \-L DIR, \-\-library-path=DIR, \-l NAME, \-\-library=NAME
Add DIR to the library search path or link NAME. With \-shared or \-dynamic, search each directory for
libNAME.so before libNAME.a; \-l:FILE searches for exactly FILE.
Command-line directories precede the library_path entries in crunch.conf.
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-relocatable
Produce a relocatable ELF object for a later link. Section groups are
preserved and duplicate COMDAT groups are discarded. Some section types,
symbol bindings, unwind layouts and options are unsupported.
.TP
.B \-o \fIfile\fR, \-\-output=\fIfile\fR
Write output to \fIfile\fR (default: a.out).
.TP
.B \-T \fIscript\fR, \-\-script=\fIscript\fR
Read a restricted linker script, supporting ENTRY, MEMORY, symbol assignments
and ALIGN expressions. Not all GNU linker script syntax is supported.
For static executable links, a non-ELF input is also treated as a script if
it contains a recognized script construct and no NUL bytes. This detection
does not validate the full script syntax.
.TP
.B \-e SYMBOL, \-\-entry=SYMBOL
Set the executable entry symbol (default: _start).
.TP
.B \-\-start-group, \-( ... \-\-end-group, \-)
Rescan archives within a group until no additional members are extracted.
.TP
.B \-u SYMBOL, \-\-undefined=SYMBOL
Force a symbol to be undefined for archive extraction.
.TP
.B \-\-require-defined=SYMBOL
Require a symbol definition, including one supplied by a direct shared input.
.TP
.B \-M FILE, \-\-Map=FILE
Write a link map. Map output is not supported with shared or relocatable output.
.TP
.B \-\-gc-sections
Enable garbage collection of unused input sections.
.TP
.B \-\-no-gc-sections
Disable section garbage collection.
.TP
.B \-\-print-gc-sections
Report discarded sections when garbage collection is enabled.
.TP
.B \-nostdlib, \-nodefaultlibs
Do not add the Crunch runtime automatically.
.TP
.B \-crt0, \-\-crt0
Prepend /usr/local/lib/crunch/crt0.o or, if absent, ./crt0.o. Fail if neither
exists. Not supported for shared, dynamic, or relocatable output.
.TP
.B \-S, \-D, \-\-strip-debug
Omit local symbols from ordinary output symbol tables.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-strip-all
Omit the ordinary symbol and string tables; shared-object dynamic symbols
remain available to the loader.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-version
Print the linker version and exit.
.TP
.B \-get
Accepted and ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print option help and exit. The short option
.B \-h
sets the shared-object SONAME and is not a help alias.
.SH AUTOMATIC RUNTIME
Executable and shared links append a readable libcrunch.a (static output)
or libcrunch.so (dynamic or shared output), unless an explicit Crunch runtime
input is already present. Relocatable output never adds a runtime.
For dynamic executables, a libcrunch-fallback.a alongside the runtime is
also added when available.
The linker searches command-line \-L directories followed by the
colon-separated library_path entries in crunch.conf, checking each directory
and its crunch subdirectory, then the configured installation runtime directory.
If no runtime is found, linking continues without one.
An explicit \-lcrunch, \-l:libcrunch.a, \-l:libcrunch.so, or runtime file
preserves its position and suppresses automatic runtime selection.
CRUNCH_CONF_FILE overrides the configured configuration filename.
An automatically selected shared runtime adds its absolute directory to
DT_RUNPATH after any explicit paths. Relative directories are resolved at
link time; directories containing ':' or '$' are rejected. Explicit runtime
inputs do not add a runtime search path automatically.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
crunch-ld -nostdlib -o program start.o
crunch-ld -r -o combined.o first.o second.o
crunch-ld -shared -h libexample.so -o libexample.so example.o
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero on success, nonzero on invalid input, unsupported options, or link failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR crunch (1),
.BR crunch-ar (1)
